.TH mbpy_wcdview 1 "1 November 2026" "MB-System 5.0" "MB-System 5.0"
.SH NAME
\fBmbpy_wcdview\fP \- Python water column viewer, an example of the mbio_py Python API.

.SH VERSION
Version 5.0

.SH SYNOPSIS
\fBmbpy_wcdview\fP
.br
[
.br
\fB\-\-input\fP=\fIfile\fP {\fB\-I\fP\fIfile\fP}
.br
\fIfile\fP
.br
\fB\-\-help\fP {\fB\-H\fP}
.br
]

.SH DESCRIPTION
\fBmbpy_wcdview\fP displays water column data, one swath at a time, read through
the MBIO library with \fBmbio_py\fP, the Python API to MBIO installed with
MB-System in \fI<prefix>/share/mbsystem/python\fP. It is written in Python with
PySide6 (Qt6 for Python) and matplotlib, and serves as an example application of
the API; \fBmbwcdview\fP(1) is the faster C++/Qt6 program for the same task.

Opening a Kongsberg *.kmwcd, *.kmall, *.wcd or *.all file finds the other file of
the survey line in the same directory (same line number, nearest file name time) and
matches the pings by ping number and time. The *.kmall or *.all supplies the
soundings, navigation, heading and transducer depth; the *.kmwcd or *.wcd the water
column. A *.all without a *.wcd supplies the water column too, if it holds any.
Either may be missing: without a *.kmall or *.all the navigation comes from the
position records of the *.kmwcd or *.wcd and depths are given below the transducer.

The window has an Open button, Save Image (twice the screen resolution, with the
file name and ping information), check boxes \fBBottom\fP (soundings of the
matching survey ping: red amplitude and dark blue phase detections, filled good,
hollow flagged), \fBWC bottom\fP (the bottom detections recorded in the water
column), \fBRay trace (SVP)\fP (samples traced through the logged sound velocity
profile; on when a profile is found) and \fBPick\fP (positions clicked in the
image go into a table that can be saved as text), the depth and amplitude ranges,
a fixed across track scale, a replay rate, and a slider that steps one swath at a
time (also the mouse wheel and the left and right arrow keys). Amplitudes are in
dB; the fill value of Kongsberg kmall data is drawn as no data before the first echo of
each beam (the near field), and later in the beam, where it is a weak echo clipped to the
lowest level, with the lowest color. In *.wcd and *.all data
the lowest value is a valid level and is drawn with the lowest color.

\fBmbpy_wcdview\fP needs Python >= 3.11, numpy, matplotlib and PySide6, and an
MB-System libmbio with \fBmb_extract_wc\fP(3). The README in
\fI<prefix>/share/mbsystem/python\fP explains the installation of these packages
and the use of the API.

